From a6141abd44a26178d27ad6abb4f523d936e15e82 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Showta Ishizaki <zakinko@snowrabbit.org>
Date: Thu, 5 Nov 2026 05:17:48 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 1/3] fs: add inode operations for NFSv4 ACLs

A filesystem whose ACLs are NFSv4 ACLs has no way to hand them to an
in-kernel user.  The only route is the system.nfs4_acl xattr, whose value
is the XDR of the protocol, and a kernel user would have to parse it.
POSIX ACLs left that shape behind for ->get_acl and ->set_acl.  Do the same
here with struct nfs4_acl, which moves to a header of its own so that a
filesystem need not take in the protocol definitions.

A principal is a uid, a gid, a special identifier or a string.  The string
is for a principal with no local id: replacing it with nobody would change
what the ACL says.

SB_I_NFS4ACL says the filesystem has an acl attribute, SB_I_NFS41ACL that it
has dacl and sacl as well.  For NFS this depends on the server, so the
operations alone cannot say it.

LSMs are asked about the xattr that presents the same object, so that a
policy does not depend on the route taken.  The set side expects the inode
lock held because nfsd_setattr() holds it.

Signed-off-by: Showta Ishizaki <zakinko@snowrabbit.org>
